readings.c1s1:
Sw. Norw. ''trampa'' = Dan. ''trampe'', tramp, stamp, tread, trample, a secondary verb, from a strong verb seen in Goth. ''anatrimpan'', tread upon (press upon); perhaps ult. akin to ''trap'', D. G. ''trappen'', tread: see ''trap''<sup>1</sup>, ''trap''<sup>2</sup>.] I. ''trans.'' 1. To tread under foot; trample.

{{smaller block|It is like unto the camamele; the more ye tread it and ''trampe'' it, the sweter it smelleth, the thicker it groweth, the better it spreddeth.
{{right|''T. Stapleton'', Fortress of the Faith (1565). (''Latham.'')}}}}

2. To tread (clothes) in water, so as to cleanse or scour them. [Scotch.]—3. To travel over on foot: as, to ''tramp'' a country.

II. ''intrans.'' 1. To walk, especially to walk with heavy step; tread; march; go on foot.

{{smaller block|How often did he . . . dread to look over his shoulder, lest he should behold some uncouth being ''tramping'' close behind him!
{{right|''Irving'', Sketch-Book, p. 425.}}}}

{{smaller block|He had ''tramped'' about the fields of the vacant farm, trying helplessly to look after things which he did not understand.
{{right|''Mrs. Oliphant'', Poor Gentleman, v.}}}}

2. To go about as a vagrant or vagabond.

'''tramp''' (tramp), ''n.'' [< ''tramp'', ''v.''] 1. The sound made by the feet in walking or marching.

{{smaller block|Then came the ''tramp'' of horse.
{{right|''Scott'', Antiquary, xxvi.}}}}

{{smaller block|The unmercifully lengthened ''tramp'' of my passing and returning footsteps.
{{right|''Hawthorne'', Scarlet Letter, Int., p. 38.}}}}

2. An excursion or journey on foot; a walk.

readings.c1s2:
¶
{{smaller block|It was his delight . . . to organize woodland ''tramps'', and to start us on researches similar to his own.
{{right|''H. B. Stowe'', Oldtown, p. 429.}}}}

{{smaller block|We shook hands with them all, men, women, and children, resuming our ''tramp'' about eleven o'clock. We still kept the main traveled road. {{right|''The Century'', XL. 615.}}}}

'''3.''' A plate of iron worn by ditchers, etc., under the hollow of the foot, to save the shoe in pressing the spade into the earth.—'''4.''' An instrument for trimming hedges.—'''5.''' An itinerant mechanic: same as ''tramper'', 2.—'''6.''' An idle vagrant; a homeless vagabond. Also ''tramper''.

{{smaller block|Another class, that of importunate sturdy ''tramps'', has been perambulating the country, composed generally of young, idle, and insolent able-bodied men, unamenable to discipline, threatening and committing lawless acts of violence in the workhouses where they obtain nightly shelter. {{right|''A. Owen'', quoted in Ribton-Turner's Vagrants and [Vagrancy, p. 267.}}}}

readings.c1s3:
¶
{{smaller block|The “sturdy beggars” who infested England two or three centuries ago reappear in our midst under the name of ''tramps.'' {{right|''J. F. Clarke'', Self-Culture, p. 280.}}}}

7. A freight-vessel that does not run in any regular line, but takes a cargo wherever the shippers desire: also used attributively, as in ''tramp steamer.'' Also called ''ocean tramp.'' [Slang.]

'''tramper''' (tram′pėr), ''n.'' [< ''tramp'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] 1. One who tramps.—2. An itinerant mechanic; a workman in search of employment.—3. An idle vagrant; a homeless vagabond; a tramp; a gipsy.

{{smaller block|They had suddenly perceived . . . a party of gipsies. . . . How the ''trampers'' might have behaved had the young ladies been more courageous must be doubtful; but such an invitation for attack could not be resisted. {{right|''Jane Austen'', Emma, xxxix.}}}}

{{smaller block|D'ye think his honour has naething else to do than to speak wi' ilka idle ''tramper'' that comes about the town? {{right|''Scott'', Heart of Mid-Lothian, xxvi.}}}}

'''tramping-drum''' (tram′ping-drum), ''n.'' In the manufacture of leather, a stuffing-wheel with hollow trunnions, through which warm air or steam is circulated into and out of the drum,

readings.c1s4:
while saturating in it a quantity of leather with oil.

'''tram-plate''' (tram′plāt), ''n.'' A flat iron plate laid as a rail: the earliest form of rail for railways.

'''trample''' (tram′pl), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''trampled'', ppr. ''trampling''. [< ME. ''trampelen'', ''tramplen'' = D. ''trampelen'' = LG. ''trampeln'' = MHG. ''trampeln'', G. ''trampeln''; a freq. of ''tramp''.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To beat or tread down by the tramping or stamping of feet, or by frequent treading; prostrate or crush by treading under foot; tread upon or tread down, literally or figuratively.

{{smaller block|Neither cast ye your pearls before swine, lest they ''trample'' them under their feet.
{{right|Mat. vii. 6.}}}}

{{smaller block|But that Humane and Diuine learning is now ''trampled'' vnder the barbarous foote of the Ottoman-Horse.
{{right|''Purchas'', Pilgrimage, p. 320.}}}}

{{smaller block|Was it not enough for thee to stoop so low for our sakes, but that thou shouldst be ''trampled'' on because thou didst it?
{{right|''Stillingfleet'', Sermons, I. vi.}}}}

{{smaller block|Squadrons of the Prince, ''trampling'' the flowers.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Princess, v.}}}}

{{smaller block|In 1869 the present ruler of Austria and Dalmatia strove . . . to ''trample'' under foot the ancient rights of the freemen of the Bocche di Cattaro.
{{right|''E. A. Freeman'', Venice, p. 236.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To tread with repeated force and shock; stamp; hence, to tramp roughshod; tread roughly or contemptuously.

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|My Muse, to some eares not vnsweet,
Tempers her words to ''trampling'' horses' feete
More oft then to a chamber-melodie.
{{right|''Sir P. Sidney'', Astrophel and Stella, lxxxiv.}}}}

{{smaller block|Certaine others . . . gathered their ananas in the Indians gardens, ''trampling'' through them without any discretion.
{{right|''Hakluyt's Voyages'', III. 320.}}}}

{{smaller block|'Tis the presumptuous and proud man alone who dares to ''trample'' on those truths which the rest of the world reverence.
{{right|''Bp. Atterbury'', Sermons, I. v.}}}}

{{smaller block|I ''trample'' on your offers and on you.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Princess, iv.}}}}

{{smaller block|Sudden and steady the music, as eight hoofs ''trample'' and thunder.
{{right|''Swinburne'', Hesperia.}}}}

'''trample''' (tram′pl), ''n.'' [< ''trample'', ''v.''] A frequent heavy or rough tread; a trampling.

{{smaller block|Under the despiteful control, the ''trample'' and spurn of all the other damned.
{{right|''Milton'', Reformation in Eng., ii.}}}}

{{smaller block|The sound is drawing close,
And speedier than the ''trample'' of speedy feet it goes.
{{right|''W. Morris'', Sigurd, ii.}}}}

'''trampler''' (tram′plėr), ''n.'' [< ''trample'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] 1. One who tramples.—2†. A lawyer.

{{smaller block|Pity your ''trampler'', sir, your poor solicitor.
{{right|''Middleton'', World Tost at Tennis.}}}}

{{smaller block|The ''trampler'' is in hast, O cleere the way,
Takes fees with both hands cause he cannot stay,
No matter wheth'r the cause be right or wrong,
So hee be payd for letting out his tongue.
{{right|''John Taylor'', Works (1630). (''Nares.'')}}}}

'''trampoose''', ''v. i.'' See ''trampous.''

'''trampot''' (tram′pot), ''n.'' [< ''tram''<sup>1</sup> + ''pot.''] In

readings.c2s2:
''milling'', the support in which the foot of the spindle is stepped.

'''trampous, trampoose''' (tram′pus, tram-pös′), ''v. i.''; pret. and pp. ''trampoused'', ''trampoosed'', ppr. ''trampousing'', ''trampoosing''. [Appar. < ''tramp'' + ''-ous'', ''-oose'', a merely capricious addition.] To tramp; walk or wander about. [Vulgar.]

{{smaller block|Some years ago I landed near to Dover,<br>And seed strange sights, ''trampoosing'' England over.
{{right|''D. Humphreys'', The Yankee in England. (''Bartlett.'')}}}}

'''tramp-pick''' (tramp′pik), ''n.'' A kind of lever of iron, about 4 feet long and 1 inch in breadth and thickness, tapering away at the lower end and having a small degree of curvature there, somewhat like the prong of a dung-fork, used for turning up very hard soils. {{smaller|It is fitted with a rest, about 18 inches from the lower end, on which the workman presses with his foot.}}

'''tramroad''' (tram′rōd), ''n.'' [Formerly also (once) ''dramroad'' (a form appar. due to the D. cognate); < ''tram''<sup>1</sup>, a rail, + ''road''.] A road in which the track for the wheels is made of pieces of wood, flat stones, or plates of iron laid in line; a tramway. See ''tramway''.

'''tram-staff''' (tram′stȧf), ''n.'' In ''milling'', a straightedge used to test the position of the spindle and millstone, and to test the surface of the stone. {{smaller|One form is called the ''red-staff'', because it is rubbed with red chalk or other coloring matter, and leaves a red mark on all prominent points it encounters in passing over the surface of the stone.}}

